1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,640
In the summer of 1762, in the royal palace of Joseon Korea, a prince was dragged into a small wooden rice chest.

2
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:16,480
The lid was shut, the lock was turned, and the man who ordered it was his own father, the king.

3
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:26,480
For eight days, the prince remained inside. No food, no water, no escape. This is not a legend.

4
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:34,480
It's one of the most documented royal murders in Asian history, recorded in the official chronicles of the Joseon dynasty.

5
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:45,520
And the question that still haunts historians today is simple. Was crown prince Sado truly insane, or was he the victim of a political conspiracy?

6
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:53,440
To understand what happened, we need to go back to 1735. The Joseon dynasty was at its peak.

7
00:00:53,440 --> 00:01:03,440
King Yongjo ruled Korea with an iron fist and an obsession with order. That year, a son was born. His name was Sado.

8
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:15,040
He was the king's second son, but the first surviving male heir. From birth, he was destined to become crown prince, the future of the dynasty.

9
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:22,480
But destiny is rarely kind. Sado's mother, Lady Yi, was a concubine of low status.

10
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:31,120
In the rigid hierarchy of Joseon Korea, this mattered enormously. The queen consort, Queen Jong-Siung, had no children.

11
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:39,440
She was Sado's legal mother, but their relationship was cold and distant. Meanwhile, King Yongjo was a man of extremes.

12
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:46,000
He was brilliant, but also paranoid, controlling and deeply insecure about his own legitimacy.

13
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:51,840
He had fought his way to the throne through political maneuvering and he feared conspiracies everywhere.

14
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:56,400
This atmosphere of suspicion would define Sado's entire life.

15
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:58,080
130.

16
00:01:58,080 --> 00:01:59,840
Four Quack

17
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:02,480
The Rise of Sado

18
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:13,520
As crown prince, Sado was expected to be perfect. He was educated in Confucian classics, trained in martial arts, and groomed for kingship from childhood.

19
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:15,520
But something was wrong.

20
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:22,000
Historical records describe Sado as a sensitive artistic child who was terrified of his father.

21
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:30,800
King Yongjo was known for his harsh discipline. He would publicly humiliate officials, and he applied the same severity to his son.

22
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:37,200
Sado's biographers note that the prince began showing signs of psychological distress as a teenager.

23
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:44,720
He would have violent outbursts. He was obsessed with clothing, changing outfits dozens of times a day.

24
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:47,680
He became terrified of thunderstorms.

25
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:55,280
Some historians believe Sado suffered from what we would now recognize as schizophrenia or bipolar disorder.

26
00:02:55,280 --> 00:03:02,320
Others argue his behavior was a reaction to years of abuse and impossible expectations.

27
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:06,800
But in 18th century Korea, mental illness was not understood.

28
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:13,680
It was seen as a moral failing, a sign of weak character or even demonic possession.

29
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:25,440
King Yongjo's response was to tighten control. He restricted Sado's movements, monitored his servants, and demanded increasingly strict adherence to Confucian rituals.

30
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:27,440
The pressure was suffocating.

31
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:38,080
Big

32
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:40,480
Seven Quack

33
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:42,880
The Escalation

34
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:48,480
By his 20s, Sado's condition had deteriorated.

35
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:53,040
Historical records from the period described disturbing incidents.

36
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:58,320
He began having violent episodes. On one occasion, he killed a unit.

37
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:06,480
On another, he attacked a servant. He became obsessed with death and would order servants to be executed for minor mistakes.

38
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:10,080
But here's where the story takes a darker turn.

39
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:17,120
Some of these incidents may have been fabricated or exaggerated by Sado's political enemies at court.

40
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:23,600
In Joseon Korea, the Crown Prince was not just a person. He was a political faction.

41
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:26,560
Sado had supporters and rivals.

42
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:32,720
The rival faction led by powerful officials had every reason to paint him as insane.

43
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:38,960
A mad prince could be removed. A sane prince with political ambitions was dangerous.

44
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,880
The most damning evidence against Sado came from his own wife, Lady Hiegyeong.

45
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:52,320
Years later, she wrote a memoir, the Han Jong-nok, that detailed Sado's madness in horrifying detail.

46
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:58,400
She described him killing servants, having hallucinations, and terrorizing the palace.

47
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:01,360
But historians have questioned her account.

48
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:07,680
Lady Hiegyeong wrote her memoir after Sado's death, when her son, Sado's son, had become king.

49
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:14,880
Her memoir served a political purpose. It justified Sado's removal and legitimized her son's succession.

50
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:21,600
Was she telling the truth? Or was she protecting her son's throne by confirming the official narrative?

51
00:05:22,160 --> 00:05:29,840
Tondorck, nine, or the rice chest. By 1762, the situation had become untenable.

52
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:38,080
King Yongjo was aging, the court was divided, and Sado's behavior, real or exaggerated,

53
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:45,600
had become impossible to ignore. On July 4, 1762, King Yongjo made his decision.

54
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:52,640
He summoned Sado to the palace. In front of the court, he accused his son of madness and

55
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:58,800
treason. Sado was not given a trial. He was not given a chance to defend himself.

56
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:05,680
Instead, he was led to a small wooden chest. A dude, used for storing rice.

57
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:12,320
It was barely large enough for a man to sit in. The lid was closed. The lock was turned.

58
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:21,760
For eight days, Sado remained inside. No food, no water. The summer heat was brutal.

59
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:30,400
On the eighth day, he died. The official record states that Sado died of illness, a transparent euphemism.

60
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:35,920
Everyone knew he had been executed by his father. But here's the final twist.

61
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:43,760
King Yongjo did not execute Sado directly. He used a method that avoided spilling royal blood,

62
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:48,960
a taboo in Confucian tradition. By locking Sado in a rice chest,

63
00:06:48,960 --> 00:06:55,200
the King could claim his son had died of illness rather than execution. It was a legal fiction.

64
00:06:55,760 --> 00:07:03,040
But in Joseon Korea, legal fictions were how power worked. After Sado's death, his son,

65
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:08,720
a young boy named Yongjo, was named Crown Prince. He would eventually become one of

66
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:13,840
Joseon's greatest kings, and he spent his reign rehabilitating his father's memory.

67
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:21,120
Yongjo built a magnificent tomb for Sado. He commissioned the Huashong fortress in Sado's honor,

68
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:26,560
and he ensured that his father's name was cleared in official records. But the question remains,

69
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:34,960
was Sado truly mad, or was he a political victim? Modern historians are divided. Some believe

70
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:40,240
Sado genuinely suffered from mental illness and was dangerous. Others argue he was a victim of

71
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:45,440
court intrigue, a sensitive man destroyed by an abusive father and political enemies.

72
00:07:46,080 --> 00:07:52,560
What we know for certain is this. A prince was locked in a rice chest by his own father. He died

73
00:07:52,560 --> 00:07:59,200
after eight days of suffering, and the official record called it illness. The truth, as always,

74
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:06,000
in history lies somewhere in between. If you found this story fascinating, subscribe for more

75
